---
import KickoffSession from "../../components/KickoffSession.astro";

const photos = [
    {
        src: "/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5390.jpg",
        alt: "Sunita Kumari speaking at the podium in a light blue blazer, with remote participants on the screen behind her.",
        caption: "On enterprise risk and who answers for a model's outcome.",
    },
];
---

<KickoffSession
    documentTitle="Know where the data sits, and who answers. | Canada2080"
    description="Sunita Kumari on AI governance, a biased mortgage model, and sovereign control of data, from her cleaned remarks at the Canada2080 kickoff."
    eyebrow="Sovereign AI · 1:20 p.m."
    headline="Know where the data sits, and who answers."
    name="Sunita Kumari"
    bio="Sunita Kumari is an AI researcher whose work centres on enterprise AI governance, cognitive stress testing, and robust system design for high-stakes deployments."
    profileUrl="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sunita-kumari-data-ai/"
    podiumSrc="/images/events/2026-kickoff/gallery/img_5390.jpg"
    podiumAlt="Sunita Kumari speaking at the podium in a light blue blazer."
    photos={photos}
>
    <p class="lede">
        Sovereign AI, she said, is who controls the environment where the model
        operates, and who answers for the outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
        Every day brings a new story of AI writing code and taking on human
        tasks. The other side is risk. Models hallucinate. Responses come back
        biased. Customer data is exposed. That is where governance starts.
    </p>
    <p>
        Governance enforces the security controls that keep a model operating
        responsibly. Sovereign AI asks who controls the environment: where the
        data resides, where the infrastructure is, where customer data sits,
        who can see it, and who is responsible for the outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
        Her example was a bank's mortgage agent that keeps denying applications
        from people with a strong credit history. If the training data is
        biased, including a preference for some locations, the responses will
        be biased too. That is the gap governance is meant to close.
    </p>
    <p>
        The controls make the output transparent. When something looks wrong,
        the system looks at the evidence and escalates to a person. If the
        person finds a problem, they ask for a retrain, and the system stays
        safe.
    </p>
    <p>
        People think governance will slow innovation. She said it is how you
        scale with confidence and trust. She remembered the early fear of
        putting a credit card online. Anti-fraud controls made the payments
        ordinary. The same shift follows once the controls are in place.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="transcript">
        <p class="transcript-note">Cleaned up transcripts.</p>
        <div class="transcript-columns">
            <div>
                <h3>Keynote</h3>
        <blockquote>
            <p>
                Hello. Good afternoon everyone. I'm Sunita. Every day we are
                hearing new stories about AI. AI is writing code. It is doing
                human tasks. But there is another side of the story, too. It is
                adding risk. Models are hallucinating. It is giving biased
                responses and customer data is exposed. And that is where AI
                governance becomes very important, and that is the journey we
                are going to explore today.
            </p>
            <p>
                AI governance enforces security controls that will make sure
                that AI models are operating responsibly. Now, who controls the
                environment where the AI operates? That is where sovereign AI
                becomes important. It will enforce meaningful controls around
                data, infrastructure, and environment, making sure where the
                data resides, where the infrastructure is, where the customer
                data is, who has access to the data, and who is responsible for
                the outcome.
            </p>
            <p>
                Let me make it real with an example. Let's say a bank has set
                up a mortgage AI agent and it is continuously denying a few
                mortgage applications even though the customer has a very good
                credit history. Now, models are trained on biased data, and
                there could be a possibility that the data is biased on
                location preferences. If the data is biased, model responses
                will also be biased, and that is the gap AI governance is
                trying to solve. It will enforce security controls, making sure
                that the output and the responses are transparent. Whenever
                there is any escalation or something is wrong, it will look at
                the evidence, and if something looks suspicious, it will
                escalate for human review. If humans find something wrong, they
                will request to retrain the model and make sure that this
                entire system operates in a safe and responsible manner.
            </p>
            <p>
                Now, people think that AI governance will slow down AI
                innovation, but it will help to scale with confidence and
                trust. Remember when credit card payments were launched, people
                were scared to put credit card details on online platforms? But
                slowly we built anti-fraud mechanisms, and everyone is
                comfortable using online payment systems. The same kind of
                shift is going to happen when we have the right governance and
                controls in place. We can scale with confidence. Thank you so
                much.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
            </div>
            <div>
                <h3 id="panel">Panel</h3>
                <p class="transcript-note">Francis Wang asked the questions.</p>
                <p class="ask">
                    What is the first distinctive thing we can do by
                    borrowing from patterns abroad? What are the patterns
                    we can accommodate in Canada?
                </p>
                <p class="transcript-note">Sunita Kumari.</p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Sovereign AI is basically enforcing meaningful control,
                        ensuring that data resides in the same country, and
                        that we have the right control. We know who accesses
                        the data, who governs it, where the server resides, and
                        who is responsible for the outcome, or who is
                        accountable if something is wrong. If we have all these
                        questions answered, I think we are in the right place
                        to implement sovereign AI.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
                <p class="ask">
                    What evidence should decision-makers demand before
                    calling an AI deployment here safe, secure, trusted, or
                    publicly beneficial?
                </p>
                <p class="transcript-note">Sunita Kumari.</p>
                <blockquote>
                    <p>
                        Governance is a continuous process. If we want to build
                        trust, we need to make sure that governance is running
                        along with every process. Every response getting
                        generated has to be governed, and that is the only way
                        we can build trust and confidence.
                    </p>
                </blockquote>
            </div>
        </div>
    </Fragment>
</KickoffSession>
